      "writtenOn": "2025-02-17T07:11:42Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "Thanks for reviewing, please let me know when your CL is ready!\n\n\u003e maybe we should have an internal non-API function on the AdapterBase to get a const reference to a Limit struct\n\nCurrently we only store the `CombinedLimits` (including `Limits v1` member together with others) within `PhysicalDevice`, so in the `AdapterBase::APIGetLimits` we actually call `mPhysicalDevice-\u003eGetLimits()` to get a const ref to that multiple times.\n\nThe problem I concern is that adapter might modify the limit values (namely `ApplyLimitTiers`) based on adapter options (`mUseTieredLimits`)([1]). As a result `APIGetLimits` may result in different adapters sharing the same physical device and get the same cont ref when calling `mPhysicalDevice-\u003eGetLimits()`.\n\nIn this case if we want a internal non-API function `AdapterBase::GetLimits()` to return the limit values for a adapter (and the API function can become its wrapper), I think we have to either: \n1) Store a normalized `CombinedLimits` within each adapter, and we can retrun the const reference of it by `const CombinedLimits\u0026 AdapterBase::GetLimits() const`, but at the cost of larger `AdapterBase` objects. Or, \n2) Just do the necessary normalization work every time we call `CombinedLimits AdapterBase::GetLimits() const` and return the new created `CombinedLimits` object instead of a reference, the overhead would be close to calling `APIGetLimits` although we don\u0027t need to construct a input or check result success.\n\n[1]:https://source.chromium.org/chromium/chromium/src/+/main:third_party/dawn/src/dawn/native/Adapter.cpp;l\u003d99",
      "parentUuid": "2a7dd879_c1ea3bf8",
      "revId": "145e0f974b877b0157e32ec3b983ee074a401937",
      "serverId": "dd02978d-1a8e-36d7-bcc0-a5723e5c0abd"
    },
